title: ADR-287 fenced literal payloads
story: dungeo
description: Real-path proof that fenced assertions match real story output headless.
---

# The banner rides along with the first command's output, so this one only
# asserts presence — the fenced assertions below start from a clean turn.
> look
[OK: any]

# The load-bearing case: this response contains a double quote, which the
# inline form CANNOT express — its payload regex is [^"]+ (parser.ts). Before
# ADR-287 there was no way to assert this text at all.
> examine welcome mat
[OK]
```
A rubber mat saying "Welcome to Dungeon!"
```

# Multi-line exact match through a fence. The blank line is REAL story output —
# a paragraph break the engine emits — and normalizeOutput preserves it, so the
# fence has to carry it too. (This is the capture-parity point ADR-282 D2 makes
# about rendered text vs channel-flattened text.)
> open mailbox
[OK]
```
You open the small mailbox.

Inside the small mailbox you see leaflet.
```

# Fenced contains: a quoted fragment of a longer response.
> examine welcome mat
[OK: contains]
```
saying "Welcome to Dungeon!"
```

# Block parity (ADR-287 D1's last grammar rule): attachment is at the assertion
# level, so a fence inside [IF] behaves exactly as one at top level. This test
# proves the inheritance rather than adding machinery for it.
[IF: location = "West of House"]
> examine welcome mat
[OK]
```
A rubber mat saying "Welcome to Dungeon!"
```
[END IF]

# The failing direction, pinned in-suite: the fence deliberately does not match
# the real response, so the assertion fails and [FAIL] records that as expected.
# (The reporter's display of fence content on failure is pinned separately, in
# transcript-tester's fenced-reporter.test.ts — a passing command prints no diff.)
> examine welcome mat
[FAIL]
[OK]
```
A rubber mat saying "Welcome to Somewhere Else!"
```
